          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Milton as Secretary for Foreign Tongues</strong><br>\nIn 1649, Milton was appointed Secretary for Foreign Tongues by the Council of State, essentially serving as a foreign affairs minister for the Commonwealth. His role included composing official statements and correspondence in Latin. Milton's proficiency in Latin was crucial for his work, which enabled England's communication with foreign powers during a tumultuous time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Loss of Sight</strong><br>\nMilton's intense work, especially his dedication to writing pamphlets supporting the Commonwealth, contributed to his eventual blindness. By 1652, he had completely lost his sight. However, his blindness did not deter him from continuing his political and literary work, dictating his verses to helpers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Paradise Lost\"</strong><br>\nMilton's most famous work, \"Paradise Lost,\" was published in 1667, after the Restoration of the monarchy. It is an epic poem that explores the biblical story of the Fall of Man and presents a complex Satan as one of the central characters. Milton's work was revolutionary in its portrayal of biblical narratives in the context of human struggle and divine providence.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
