          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>After the Restoration</strong><br>\nFollowing the Restoration in 1660, Milton was arrested as a supporter of the Commonwealth but was soon released. He continued to write, completing \"Paradise Regained\" and \"Samson Agonistes,\" which reflected his personal despair but also his unyielding faith and intellect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Milton's Prose and Philosophy</strong><br>\nMilton wrote a significant amount of prose, expressing his philosophical beliefs about individual freedom, divorce, and the right to overthrow tyrannical governments. His writings go beyond mere advocacy, delving into discussions of free will, truth, and morality, which influenced both contemporaneous and later political thought.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nJohn Milton died on November 8, 1674, leaving behind a legacy that extends beyond his contribution to literature. His commitment to individual liberty and free speech made a lasting impact on Enlightenment thinkers. His literary works, particularly \"Paradise Lost,\" have been celebrated for their linguistic richness and complex treatment of theology and human free will.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
